_and assume that \(\mathbf{u}\) is such that \(r_{h}\mathbf{u}\) is well defined. Then there exists \(p_{h}\) in \(\Theta_{h}\) such that_

\[r_{h}\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{grad}\,p_{h}.\]

Proof.: As \(\mathbf{curl}\,\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{0}\), Remark 5.6 implies that

\[\mathbf{curl}\,r_{h}\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{0}\quad\text{in each }\kappa.\]

